class: middle

# Plaintext Backups (BIP-39)

Pros:
### Minimal complexity: something you have + [ something you know ]
### Industry-wide support

Cons:
### Changes require complete setup/testing, on-chain activity
### Multiple backups may be a liability
### Transition from "securely-stored" to "in-use" may be a liability

???

Pros:
The simple scheme of BIP-39 mnemonic and/or passphrase has been proven by many over time.  It is supported by so many projects that it's safe to say that it will survive far into the future.

Cons:
But to make any change to either mnemonic or passphrase requires full wallet setup. Generate new secrets, setup new wallet, and backup(s), verify addresses, test signing, distribute the backups, and then move/sweep each UTXO on-chain to the new setup -- with the all of the hastle/expense/privacy-risks that this entails.

But are your backups secure?  Do you have a safe place to hide them?  Will you know if they've been disturbed?  How many duplicates can you securely store? ...and will you remember how-many -- and where they all are?

Not everyone has true "security" where they call home, or at their work-place.  Most are actually co-habiting with others, some are jet-set -- on the go, or couch-surfing, or in a colocation.  Even if you own or rent an independent home, do you know every invitee of your family members, and do you believe that others living or invited into your home wouldn't be able to identify a plaintext backup for what it might represent?

---
class: middle

# Encrypted Backups

Pros:
### Modify backups at any time w/o altering setup, w/o onchain activity
### Multiple backups; including ones only heirs/others can decrypt
### Plaintext secret revealed only on-device

Cons:
### Complexity, yet another "secret" to keep secure
### Weak-keys, poor implementation, unknowns
### KEF: Limited support - Krux and 3rdIteration's SeedSigner fork

???

Pros:
As long as your wallet setup is still secure, you can modify encryption keys without altering that setup, and without any on-chain activity.

Since decryption to plaintext occurs inside a "trusted" signing device, secrets are not exposed to the real world while using them.

You can make as many encrypted backups of the same plaintext secrets as you like.  Including ones for your heirs that only they can decrypt (even multi-layered to mitigate risk of an heir using a weak key). 

Cons:
With encryption we're adding complexity to our setup without being able to avoid "Priority #1", our fundamental responsibility to address the question "Can you keep a secret?".  Arguably, now we have even more secrets to keep.

And whenever there is a new "secret" to keep, users may become "lazy" picking their encryption key, leading to a false sense of security.  "My backups are all encrypted!!!", but "abc" is useless as an encryption key.  While imperfect humans might write great software, we also write bugs, so poor implementation can be a problem at both ends: during encryption as well as decryption.  While AES is finally a mature adult, assume that we won't learn of its latest flaws until it's too late.

For KEF specifically, it is rather new -- first released in September 2023 and extended to its current form only recently. KEF is supported ONLY by Krux and YTCryptoGuide's SeedSigner fork. Without further adoption, users who rely on a KEF encrypted backup may someday find their secure backup in perfect shape, remembering the decryption key perfectly, and have trouble finding tools to decrypt their backup -- or at least to do so in a safe computing environment like an air-gapped signing device.  No matter how much someone loves KEF today, they'd need their head examined if relying 100% on any KEF backup; future adoption of KEF could ease that concern.


